we're learning more about the risks of  the brain injury cte in athletes the  family of former nfl star demaryius  thomas revealed yesterday the signs of  cte when he died in december but it  isn't just football that carries risks  of brain injury here on cbs mornings  we've been looking into how the  condition could affect soccer players  yesterday retired u.s soccer star bruce  murray shared his neurological symptoms  and his fight to protect other players  this morning the family of another  player scott vermillion is talking about  his cte diagnosis which came after he  died nearly two years ago at age 44. it  was the first public diagnosis of the  disease in a former major league soccer  player first on cbs morning's chief  medical correspondent dr john lepuc  talked with vermilion's family who said  they learned about cte warning signs  after it was already too late  scott vermillion played five seasons for  major league soccer teams  including his hometown kansas city  wizards and dc united  he was also a devoted father to his two  children ava grace and braden what was  it like with your dad when you were  younger when you were growing up he was  quick-witted he was compassionate when  we were younger i would say he was more  like outgoing and just like happier as a  person at age five vermillion started  playing soccer from the very start he  was a standout how many goals would he  score i mean  by half time he'd have six or seven this  is where scott played it is scott's  father dave vermillion watched his son  reach the heights of elite soccer at age  26 an ankle injury ended his career but  scott seemed to be moving forward with a  new career in nursing looking back was  there a moment when you noticed that his  personality changed  yeah there was something off about the  way he was acting and did you ever link  the changes in his personality and in  his behavior to his soccer career no  dave vermillion says his son started  drinking too much and abusing the  painkillers he was taking for his injury  over the next decade scott tried rehab  several times but kept relapsing and  what would he say he would say dad i  have to take care of this myself  and i said well  you're not  taking care of yourself christmas at my  mom papas yeah  the change in vermilion's behavior was  obvious to his ex-wife cami and their  two children the things that he promised  that he would be there for  were like  life-changing big moments for us he said  he was going to go to braden's  graduation right and didn't show up  because he said he had work  how did you find out that he had died i  first found out it was on christmas in  december 2020 a friend found scott  vermillion in his house  he was not breathing i stepped out there  and he called me up and i  kind of knew like right away everyone  was crying vermilion had died from a  combination of alcohol and muscle  relaxers  the family began to suspect that the  game he loved had damaged his brain  they turned to dr ann mckee director of  boston university's center for chronic  traumatic encephalopathy or cte and that  wasn't normal for a 44 year old man dr  mckee found an abnormal protein  suggestive of cte in areas of  vermilion's brain that control memory  and organization of thought it's cte and  only cte that was the only thing that  was wrong with his brain dr mckee  believes concussions are not the only  cause of cte those balls are coming from  a great distance at a tremendous  velocity and that impacts the head and  causes  rapid acceleration deceleration and  rotation to the brain the professionals  have longer years of exposure but kids  kids who play high school sports they're  at risk for these changes dave  vermillion says during scott's many  years of playing soccer he had only one  concussion  substance abuse and unexplained  personality changes are early signs of  cte they can't control their brain and  they start isolating themselves from  people who care about them we see  hundreds of cases why aren't we doing  more to prevent this major league soccer  which governs the professional teams  vermillion played for declined our  request for an on-camera interview when  we asked about their protocols for blows  to the head that don't cause concussions  the league said it follows the most  up-to-date science and cited its medical  spotter program to identify potential  head injuries and removing from play any  player with a suspected head injury  for scott vermillion's family the report  from boston university brings some  answers  but also regrets  i wish  in those four years that  this was going on  i could have spent more time with him  and  been a little bit again more  compassionate there's definitely some  closure there though yeah  how so  just knowing the fact that his brain  wasn't like  processing the way a normal person would  process it and it's not like he  intentionally was doing this he wanted  to get help he wanted to be there for us  he was just sick and had this  disease  dr john lepux joins us right now  what is one of the biggest things you  want us to take away from this you know  the gift that the vermillion family is  giving us and that bruce murray and his  family gave us yesterday is awareness  they had no idea that the changes in  personality and memory and behavior had  anything to do with a sport and i think  if people are out there watching right  now if you are somebody involved in a  sport or you know somebody you notice a  personality change behavior change get  help see a doctor and and think about  going to a neurologist  is it about therapy is it about rehab of  the mind by get help what do you mean  well that first of all try to get a  diagnosis and we talked yesterday about  the importance of having a way of  diagnosing it before the person is dead  right now cte chronic traumatic  encephalopathy has to be on biopsy we  need ways of diagnosing it beforehand  and then maybe figure out ways of  preventing it thank you we appreciate  you
 

